# Case Analysis: Kwok Cheuk Kin and Another v Director of Lands and Others [2021] HKCA 54

## 1. Summary of Facts

The case concerned a constitutional challenge to the New Territories Small House Policy ("Small House Policy"), an administrative policy approved by the Executive Council in 1972 and implemented since December of that year.

Under the Small House Policy, an indigenous villager in the New Territories may apply for permission to erect, once in his lifetime, a small house on a suitable site within his own village. The eligibility criteria require that the applicant be an indigenous villager who is a male person at least 18 years old and descended through the male line from a resident in 1898 of a recognized village. The applicant must not have previously received any small house grant, the site must be within the "V" Zone and the environs or village extension area of a recognized village, the site must not be affected by impending development, and there must be no substantiated local objections.

A small house grant takes three forms: (a) free building licence (on private land); (b) private treaty grant (on Government land); and (c) land exchange (surrender and re-grant). For free building licence or exchange, the land must be registered in the applicant's name. Premiums vary: nil for free building licence on the applicant's own land; about two-thirds of full market rate for private treaty grant of Government land; and nil for the private land portion in an exchange, with a concessionary rate for the Government land portion.

The rights of a male New Territories indigenous villager to apply for a small house grant under the policy have been commonly referred to as "the Ding Rights."

The applicants brought judicial review proceedings challenging the constitutionality of the Small House Policy on three grounds: (1) discrimination against persons of "non-indigenous" status contrary to Articles 25 and 39 of the Basic Law and/or Article 22 of the Hong Kong Bill of Rights; (2) discrimination against female indigenous villagers based on sex; and (3) failure by the Government to manage land for the benefit of all Hong Kong residents contrary to Article 7 of the Basic Law.

The respondents and the interested party invoked Article 40 of the Basic Law, arguing that the Ding Rights are lawful traditional rights and interests of the New Territories indigenous inhabitants, thus rendering the Small House Policy constitutional and lawful.

Chow J, in his judgment dated 8 April 2019, found that the Small House Policy is discriminatory based on social origin/birth and/or sex in contravention of Article 25 and Article 22 of the Bill of Rights, absent justification satisfying the proportionality test. He held that of the three forms of land grant, only free building licence could be regarded as a lawful traditional right or interest of the New Territories indigenous inhabitants. Private treaty grant and exchange could not be so regarded as they did not satisfy the tracing exercise he propounded. He declared the Small House Policy unconstitutional insofar as it permitted private treaty grant and exchange, and declared the relevant exemption in the Sex Discrimination Ordinance unconstitutional to that extent.

All parties appealed.

## 2. Summary of Arguments

**The Applicants' Arguments:**

Mr Martin Lee SC, for the applicants, argued that the Judge erred in not holding that the entire Small House Policy had not been rendered lawful by Article 40, which only protects lawful traditional rights and interests of New Territories indigenous inhabitants—that is, rights and interests not against the laws of Hong Kong, including the Basic Law, in particular Articles 25 and 39, and Article 22 of the Bill of Rights.

The applicants contended that free building licence is not a lawful traditional right or interest under Article 40 for three reasons: First, "lawful" refers to differential treatment which is justified, and therefore does not infringe Articles 25 and 39 and Article 22 of the Bill of Rights. Second, the Ding Rights are not "rights or interests" as the Small House Policy only confers a right to apply under a completely discretionary government policy. Third, the Ding Rights are not "traditional" in that they are not traceable to pre-1898; the free building licence under the Small House Policy has nothing in common with what existed pre-1898, save that the licence is free.

The applicants also argued that "lawful" means lawful both according to Qing law before the New Territories Lease in 1898 and according to the domestic laws of Hong Kong in 1997, and not inconsistent with the Basic Law. They submitted that "lawful" in Article 40 means lawful according to both Qing Law before the New Territories Lease and the domestic laws of Hong Kong after 1997.

**The Respondents' Arguments:**

Mr Benjamin Yu SC, for the respondents, contended that the entirety of the Ding Rights of New Territories indigenous inhabitants under the Small House Policy are protected under Article 40. The Judge erred in concluding that Article 40 only protects grants under the Small House Policy by way of free building licence but not private treaty grant or exchange.

The respondents argued that private treaty grant is lawful as it is part of a well-publicized administrative policy protected and recognized by Hong Kong law. Further, the Basic Law, including Articles 25, 39, 40 and 122, must be read together congruously. The objective pre-enactment materials also show the drafters of the Basic Law had discussed the issue and resolved to entrench the rights and interests of the New Territories indigenous inhabitants notwithstanding that those rights may be discriminatory. Consequently, the Small House Policy and indeed the private treaty grant cannot be regarded as unlawful because of its discriminatory nature.

Private treaty grant is traditional as the rights and interests thereunder are traceable back to, in that they capture or reproduce the essential features of, the rights and interests enjoyed by villagers before the British took the New Territories Lease in 1898. Alternatively, there can be no doubt that one of the objects and purposes of Article 40 was the protection of lawful and traditional rights and interests of the New Territories indigenous inhabitants, and that the rights and interests of the New Territories indigenous inhabitants under the Small House Policy was, at the time when Article 40 was drafted, objectively regarded as one of the lawful and traditional rights and interests of the New Territories indigenous inhabitants.

For exchange, the respondents argued that it is an administrative instrument to overcome practical difficulties faced by an eligible New Territories indigenous inhabitant and a means to give effect to the rights and interests under free building licence, which the Judge had declared to be constitutional. It was therefore wrong for him to have declared it unconstitutional or required it to pass the four constituent elements of Article 40 separately.

The respondents also complained that the Judge erred in not refusing relief and dismissing the judicial review on the basis of serious and unexplained delay and the applicants' lack of standing.

**The Interested Party's Arguments:**

Lord Pannick QC, for the interested party, argued that "the lawful and traditional rights and interests of the New Territories indigenous inhabitants" under Article 40 refer to the rights and interests enjoyed by them at the time when the Basic Law was drafted and prior to its coming into force in 1997. The term is descriptive. The entirety of the Small House Policy, inclusive of private treaty grant and exchange, was entitled to mandatory protection under Article 40.

This is supported by the theme of continuity after resumption of sovereignty enshrined in the Basic Law generally, and for Article 40 specifically. Article 40 is not concerned with the state of affairs in 1898. The Judge erred in concluding that it was necessary to engage in a historical tracing exercise to determine whether the relevant right or interest captured or reproduced the essential feature of a right or interest enjoyed by the New Territories indigenous inhabitants prior to the New Territories Lease in 1898.

Having regard to the theme of continuity, the admissible extrinsic materials, and Article 122, there is no support for the view that the Basic Law intended to constitutionally enshrine free building licence but not private treaty grant and exchange under Article 40. On the contrary, the plain intent was to provide for mandatory protection for all forms of land grant under the Small House Policy.

The interested party further argued that even on his own interpretation of Article 40, the Judge erred in concluding that private treaty grant was not a protected form of land grant under Article 40. The Judge ought to have concluded that, on the evidence, the essential feature of private treaty grant, namely, the right or interest to acquire land for building a house, was traceable to those enjoyed by the New Territories indigenous inhabitants before 1898. The Judge likewise erred in declaring exchange unconstitutional. It should be regarded as a derivative of free building licence or alternatively an administrative practice to give effect to free building licence or private treaty grant which satisfies the requirements of Article 40 or does not need itself to do so.

The interested party also complained that the Judge erred in not refusing relief and dismissing the judicial review on the basis of serious and unexplained delay and the applicants' lack of standing.

## 3. Legal Principles (Ratio Decidendi)

### A. Contextual and Purposive Approach to Constitutional Interpretation

The Court of Appeal affirmed that the courts adopt a contextual and purposive approach in construing a provision of the Basic Law, citing Ng Ka Ling & Others v Director of Immigration (1999) 2 HKCFAR 4 at pp.28-29; Director of Immigration v Chong Fung Yuen (2001) 4 HKCFAR 211 at p.224D-G; Vallejos v Commissioner of Registration (2013) 16 HKCFAR 45 at [76]-[77]; and Comilang Milagros Tecson v Director of Immigration (2019) 22 HKCFAR 59 at [60]. The context and purpose of a provision of the Basic Law were established at the time when it was enacted in 1990. The materials for identifying them include other provisions of the Basic Law as well as other relevant extrinsic materials including the Joint Declaration, the state of the legislation at or about the time of the Joint Declaration and the enactment of the Basic Law and other pre-enactment materials brought into existence prior to or contemporaneous with the enactment of the Basic Law.

### B. Historical Context of Article 40

The Court conducted an extensive historical survey, identifying twelve contextual matters relevant to the interpretation of Article 40:

**(i) Status of New Territories indigenous inhabitants originated from 1898.** The concept of an inhabitant of Hong Kong as a New Territories indigenous inhabitant only came into existence in 1898. The term "New Territories" was first used by the Colonial Government to describe the territory in the San On District leased to the British Government under the New Territories Lease. The term "NTII" must have been used to distinguish the inhabitants then residing in the leased territory from the rest of the Hong Kong population. When time passes, the term also includes the descendants of the first generation of New Territories indigenous inhabitants. In short, NTII as a status has its origin in 1898.

**(ii) Stated intention to respect customs and usages.** It was the stated intention of the Colonial Government in the early days of the New Territories to respect the customs and usages of its inhabitants, as seen from the Governor's Proclamation of 9 April 1899 and his speech given on the hoisting of the Flag on 17 April 1899.

**(iii) Recognition of importance of descent in the male line.** The Colonial Government had recognized in the early days of the New Territories that descent in the male line was of paramount importance in the life of the Chinese.

**(iv) But always subject to law.** Such intention and recognition of the Colonial Government were necessarily subject to the general laws applicable to the whole of Hong Kong, including the New Territories. The Governor's Proclamations did not have any effect or force in law: Winfat Enterprises (HK) Co Ltd v Attorney General [1984] HKLR 32, at pp.43-44, per Roberts CJ.

**(v) General laws applicable in the New Territories.** By the Governor's Proclamation of 8 April 1899, all laws and ordinances in force in the Colony of Hong Kong should take effect in the New Territories also.

**(vi) New system of land holding in the New Territories.** From its early days, the Colonial Government had taken active steps to swiftly introduce a new system of land holding in the New Territories, which was fundamentally different from the one in the pre-Colonial era. The previous land holding system involved "common tenure" and "customary tenure" based on custom or traditions, with land notionally divided into "Subsoil" and "Topsoil." The new system was established through the New Territories (Land Court) Ordinances of 1900, which declared all land in the New Territories to be the property of the Crown, and through Block Crown Leases. The legal position was summarized by Lord Diplock in Winfat Enterprise (PC) at p.745A-C: for the common or customary Chinese tenure under which the inhabitants had previously occupied their land, there was substituted a leasehold interest of 99 years less three days.

**(vii) General policy with regard to small houses before 1972.** The Colonial Government's general policy with regard to small houses in the New Territories before 1972 recognized the NTIIs' long-held custom to build houses in their village for their own occupation on their own land held under the Block Crown Lease since 1898. This tradition was officially endorsed by the Executive Council in 1959. The recognition of such custom was put into practice by the Colonial Government granting permission to build village houses by way of free building licences, surrender/re-grant, and private treaty grant when applied. None of these were new when adopted by the Small House Policy in 1972.

**(viii) Basis of the Small House Policy.** The Small House Policy plainly continued the previous practice of small house grant and had in fact elevated it into a formal government policy. As the previous practice was based on the Government's recognition of the NTII's customs since 1898 to build village houses for their own occupation on their own land, the Small House Policy must have also operated on the same as its very basis.

**(ix) Recognition in legislation.** The government's previous practice with respect to small houses before 1972 was recognized in legislation, including the Buildings Ordinance and its application to the New Territories. The Small House Policy after 1972 is also recognized in legislation, including the Sex Discrimination Ordinance and the Government Rent (Assessment and Collection) Ordinance.

**(x) Preservation of small house grants in Annex III of the Joint Declaration.** Paragraphs 1 and 2 of Annex III to the Joint Declaration evidenced an agreement reached by both Governments to preserve and protect small houses granted before the resumption of sovereignty on 1 July 1997 and thereafter. Both Governments envisaged that the Small House Policy under which small house grants are made would continue after the coming into force of the Joint Declaration and beyond 1 July 1997.

**(xi) Drafting materials.** The contemporaneous drafting materials of Article 40 between 1986 and 1989 showed that there was a sharp division of contemporary opinions on whether the existing rights and interests of the New Territories indigenous inhabitants (including the Small House Policy) ought to be preserved or protected after 1 July 1997. The objections included arguments based on the right to equality or the right against discrimination. On the other hand, those who supported the preservation and protection argued that those rights and interests were "historical problems," or had "historical origins," or arose due to the "special circumstances of New Territories," or were based on "a few hundred years of traditional custom or practices" which ought to be respected. It was generally thought or assumed that the Ding Rights formed part of the existing traditional rights and interests of the New Territories indigenous inhabitants. It was eventually decided that the "traditional rights and interests," without further elaboration, of the New Territories indigenous inhabitants would be protected by the Basic Law via Article 40.

**(xii) No legal challenge.** The Small House Policy had never been subject to any legal challenge since 1972 until the proceedings below. Until the Judgment below, the Small House Policy in its entirety had all along been recognized as lawful in the legal system before and after 1997, notwithstanding its inherent discriminatory nature and the fact that the Bill of Rights came into force in 1991.

### C. Purpose and Objective of Article 40

The Court held that in ascertaining the purpose and objective of Article 40, its specific historical context is instructive. The historical factual matrix showed that the New Territories indigenous inhabitants are treated as a class of their own when it comes to their traditional rights and interests and the protection that they enjoy. Those facts must lie at the centre of the court's deliberations on the purpose and objective of Article 40.

The Court held that by adopting the term "NTIIs," the drafters must have 1898 in mind. At that time, the NTIIs already enjoyed certain rights and interests, derived from the customs and usages practiced in the New Territories in the pre-Colonial era, which were unique to them because of their status and the assurances given to them on behalf of the Colonial Administration. Subject to and if necessary as modified by laws in terms of their recognition, their form and the manner in which they were exercised and enjoyed, when such rights and interests were passed down from the first generation to their descendants, they became the NTIIs' lawful traditional rights and interests as recognized in the legal system.

As evident from the drafting materials, the drafters of the Basic Law were presented with certain specific rights and interests, said but not necessarily accepted by all to be traditional and lawful, which the NTIIs presently enjoyed and would continue to enjoy. In drafting Article 40, the drafters plainly aimed at protecting those extant rights and interests of the NTIIs which were recognized in the legal system as traditional and lawful at the time.

### D. The "Traditional" Element

The Court held that on the "traditional" aspect, the drafters clearly did not intend to protect some rights and interests which might have been traditional but had already become extinct at the time or which might be asserted to be traditional sometime in the future. Whether a right or interest is traditional for the purpose of Article 40 is to be determined by reference to the state of affairs in April 1990. So Article 40 does not entail the tracing exercise advocated by the respondents and accepted by the Judge for the purpose of determining if an asserted right or interest is a traditional right or interest of the NTIIs entitling its protection.

The Court noted that the Judge at [55] of the Judgment cited Tse Kwan Sang and Koon Ping Leung in support of the tracing exercise. However, it should be noted that most of the historical and drafting materials pertaining to the construction of Article 40 were not presented before the courts in those cases, as they were here in these appeals. Thus the two cases should not be regarded as authoritative on the "traditional" aspect of Article 40.

Further, the Court held that human experience shows that traditional rights and interests might evolve when they were passed down from one generation to another without however losing their essential features. Or that there might be occasional gaps and disruptions here and there without however really breaking their continuity or rendering them extinct. The drafters must have clearly intended to protect a traditional right and interest of the NTIIs although it might not be enjoyed in exactly the same manner in its pre-1898 form provided it retains its essential or core features. Such intention accords with the cardinal principle that a constitutional provision must be interpreted to protect the essence of the right in question from impairment: see W v Registrar of Marriages (2013) 16 HKCFAR 112 at [65]-[67]. The drafters must have also intended to protect a traditional right or interest despite some occasional gaps or disruptions which did not break its continuity or render it extinct.

### E. The "Lawful" Element

The Court held that on the "lawful" aspect, the drafters intended to protect existing traditional rights and interests which were recognized as lawful in the legal system at the time. Contrary to the applicants' argument, the drafters did not intend to leave open the question of lawfulness to be determined by the courts after the Basic Law came into effect by reference to the Qing law, or the law before 1 July 1997 or thereafter. For it would result in much uncertainty and render the promise to give constitutional protection to the NTIIs' lawful traditional rights and interests already included in Article 40 as early as April 1990 inefficacious, which clearly is not what the drafters intended.

The Court held that "lawful traditional rights and interests of the NTIIs" in Article 40 mean those which are, as a matter of historical fact, recognized to be the NTIIs' lawful traditional rights and interests in the Hong Kong legal system at the time of the promulgation of the Basic Law on 4 April 1990. Applying the theme of continuity, Article 40 continues and elevates the recognition and protection to a constitutional level for such lawful traditional rights and interests of NTIIs after 1 July 1997.

### F. The Ding Rights as Lawful Traditional Rights and Interests

The Court held that it is beyond doubt that the Ding Rights were as a matter of fact recognized in the Hong Kong legal system as lawful traditional rights and interests of the NTIIs during the drafting stage despite the heated debates about their discriminatory nature. They were recognized as traditional in that they originated from and retained the essence of the NTIIs' pre-1898 customs to build a house for their own occupation on their own land despite the change of the land holding system after 1898 and the disruptions caused by World War II. They were recognized as lawful in that they were embedded in legislation and in the form of a government policy, giving rise to legal consequences enforceable in court. They were not made unlawful by any statute, nor were they subject to any legal challenge or court decision denouncing them to be unlawful. They had all along been recognized and accepted to be lawful.

The Ding Rights continued to be recognized in the legal system as the NTIIs' lawful traditional rights and interests when the Basic Law was promulgated in April 1990. It therefore falls within the ambit of Article 40 and merits the constitutional protection in full despite its inherently discriminatory nature. Since the protection has been elevated to the constitutional level, Article 40 shields them from any challenge against their legality based on discrimination or other constitutional grounds including the equality provisions such as Articles 25 and 39 and Article 22 of the Bill of Rights. If such challenge were allowed, it would defeat the whole purpose of Article 40.

### G. Coherence Principle and Reading Article 40 with Articles 120 and 122

The Court held that the coherence principle mandates that Article 40 must be read together with Articles 120 and 122. It is because an article of the Basic Law should be read with and in light of other articles, especially those addressing the same or similar subject matter, to produce a harmonious and congruous instead of a discordant meaning: Chief Executive of HKSAR v President of the Legislative Council [2017] 1 HKLRD 460, per Cheung CJHC (as he then was) at [51].

Article 120, which gives effect to paragraph 1 of Annex III, stipulates that all leases of land granted, decided upon or renewed before the establishment of the Hong Kong Special Administrative Region which extend beyond 30 June 1997, and all rights in relation to such leases, shall continue to be recognized and protected under the law of the Region. It confirms that all land grants, including small house grants made pursuant to the Small House Policy irrespective of their form, made up to 30 June 1997 shall be continued to be recognized and protected under the law of the Hong Kong Special Administrative Region.

Article 122, which implements paragraph 2 of Annex III, provides that in the case of old schedule lots, village lots, small houses and similar rural holdings, where the property was on 30 June 1984 held by, or, in the case of small houses granted after that date, where the property is granted to, a lessee descended through the male line from a person who was in 1898 a resident of an established village in Hong Kong, the previous rent shall remain unchanged so long as the property is held by that lessee or by one of his lawful successors in the male line.

The Court noted several points: (1) When Article 122 refers to small houses granted after 30 June 1984, it is not limited to those made up to 30 June 1997 but clearly extends to those made thereafter. (2) The benefit with regards to small houses granted after 30 June 1984 must have been predicated upon the continued validity of the small house grants so made after 1 July 1997. (3) The reference to "a lessee descended through the male line from a person who was in 1898 a resident of an established village in Hong Kong" incorporates the definition found in the Small House Policy, a strong indicator that small house grants mean those made pursuant to the Small House Policy. (4) Subsequently, the Government Rent (Assessment and Collection) Ordinance, in particular, section 4 applying to small houses, was enacted to implement Article 122.

Reading Articles 120 and 122 together, insofar as small house grants made pursuant to the Small House Policy are concerned, irrespective of the form and whether they are made before or on 30 June 1997, they will continue to be recognized and protected under the law of the HKSAR. When Article 40 is construed together with Articles 120 and 122, the Ding Rights must fall within the scope of NTIIs' lawful traditional rights and interests. Any contrary construction would render Articles 120 and 122, insofar as small house grants are concerned, quite meaningless.

### H. CEDAW Reservations and Coherence with Equality Provisions

The Court held that both the UK and PRC Governments are parties to the 1979 Convention on the Elimination of All Forms of Discrimination against Women. Both Governments were evidently aware of the inherent discriminatory nature of the Small House Policy against female when they made the Joint Declaration, which might fall foul of the CEDAW. Thus on 14 October 1996, the UK Government entered into a reservation with respect to its application to Hong Kong whereby laws applicable in the New Territories which enable male indigenous villagers to exercise certain rights in respect of property and which provide for rent concessions in respect of land or property held by indigenous persons or their lawful successors through the male line will continue to be applied. The PRC Government made a similar reservation on 10 June 1997.

The Court held that these reservations with respect to the application of CEDAW is a weighty aid to the interpretation of the Basic Law and the Bill of Rights: see Comilang Milagros Tecson v Director of Immigration [2018] 2 HKLRD 534, per Cheung CJHC (as he then was) at [5]; and ZN v Secretary for Justice [2018] 3 HKLRD 778, per Lam VP at [202]. They seek to ensure the continued application of the laws which enable male indigenous villagers to exercise certain rights in respect of property, which clearly include the Ding Rights, after 1 July 1997 despite their discriminatory nature against female. Article 40 must be construed consistently with these reservations: see Comilang (CFA) at [24]-[35]. Under such construction, the NTIIs must be entitled to exercise the Ding Rights, despite its discriminatory nature against female, as their lawful traditional rights and interests by virtue of the reservations.

While the CEDAW reservations render a discrimination challenge against the Ding Rights based on sex impermissible, it must also inform the construction of Article 40 in the context of a discrimination challenge based on other grounds, such as birth or social origin. The reason is again coherence.

In Comilang (CFA), Ribeiro and Fok PJJ held that the immigration reservation contained in section 11 of the Hong Kong Bill of Rights Ordinance excluded from the scope of the provisions of the Bill of Rights given constitutional effect by Article 39, immigration legislation governing entry into, stay in and departure from Hong Kong and the application of such legislation. Further (and relevantly for present purposes), the Basic Law must be interpreted as a coherent whole, consistently with section 11. Therefore, section 11 was not confined to rights in the Bill of Rights but extended by necessary implication to cognate rights in the Basic Law in the specific immigration context.

Consistent with the CEDAW reservations excluding a discrimination challenge based on sex, Article 40 must be construed to also exclude a discrimination challenge based on other grounds, such as birth or social origin, available in the Basic Law or the Bill of Rights. Such construction ensures that a coherent constitutional scheme is laid down in Article 40 to protect the Ding Rights as the NTIIs' lawful traditional rights and interests.

### I. The Ding Rights as "Rights and Interests"

The Court rejected Mr Lee's argument that since the NTIIs at most can only have a right to apply for a small house grant under the Small House Policy, the Ding Rights fall short of rights and interests under Article 40 whatever they might entail.

The Court held that it is trite that as a government policy, the Small House Policy creates legal obligations on the part of the Government to act upon it when faced with an application by an eligible NTII under the doctrine of legitimate expectation as explained by the Court of Final Appeal in Ng Siu Tung & Others v Director of Immigration (2002) 5 HKCFAR 1, at [92]; or by a related but free-standing principle articulated by Laws LJ in R (Nadarajah) v Secretary of State for the Home Department [2005] EWCA Civ 1363 at [68]; quoted with approval by Lord Wilson JSC in Mandalia v Secretary of State for the Home Department [2015] 1 WLR 5456, at [29]: "Where a public authority has issued a promise or adopted a practice which represents how it proposes to act in a given area, the law will require the promise or practice to be honoured unless there is good reason not to do so. What is the principle behind this proposition? It is not far to seek. It is said to be grounded in fairness, and no doubt in general terms that is so. I would prefer to express it rather more broadly as a requirement of good administration, by which public bodies ought to deal straightforwardly and consistently with the public."

Corresponding to the Government's obligations, an eligible NTII must have a right to the determination of his application in accordance with the Small House Policy. It is plainly within the ambit of rights and interests for the purpose of Article 40.

### J. Alternative Case Based on the Judge's Approach

Even adopting the Judge's approach on Article 40, including the four constituent elements and the tracing exercise, the Court would have come to the same conclusion.

On the "traditional" element, if contrary to the Court's view, tracing is necessary, the Court agreed with the Judge that a right or interest could properly be regarded as "traceable" if it captures or reproduces the essential feature(s) of a right or interest that was enjoyed by the NTIIs before the commencement of the New Territories Lease. For the reasons that the Judge gave, the Court agreed with him that the Ding Rights in the form of free building licence is a traceable right for the purpose of Article 40. However, the Court thought he erred when he did not reach the same conclusion for private treaty grant and exchange.

The Court held that land in the village areas in the New Territories after 1898 were sold at concessionary rates by way of public auction from around 1901 to 1904 or 1909; public treaty grant from around 1909 to a few years after World War II; restricted village auctions (which were effectively private treaty grants) from 1950s to 1972; and private treaty grants since 1972. The essential or core features of a NTII's right to apply for new land to build a house under the "customary tenure" were preserved by the successive ways of disposal of land in the New Territories.

The fact that the land was sold at the concessionary rate whatever the ways of disposal might be over the years is significant. The concessionary rate convincingly showed that private treaty grant was intended to enable an eligible NTII who did not own any agricultural land to, subject to the Government's consent, acquire building land at a price commensurate to the costs he would have to incur had he acquired agricultural land and applied to convert it into building land via free building licence. Put in the simplest terms, he would be placed in the same position as if he were an owner of an Old Scheduled lot who could apply to build a house via free building licence.

The Court agreed with Mr Yu and Lord Pannick that the rights and interests enjoyed by an eligible NTII under a private treaty grant capture or reproduce the essential or core features of an eligible villager's right to apply for new land to build a house under the "customary tenure" in the pre-Colonial era.

For exchange, the Court also accepted the submissions of Mr Yu and Lord Pannick that it is just a derivative of free building licence.

On the "lawful" element, the Court agreed with the Judge.

### K. Relief Issue

In light of the Court's conclusion on the Construction Issue, the Relief Issue became academic. The Court only stated brief reasons why it differed from the Judge.

The Court held that the length of delay in commencing the proceedings below is not only serious; it is phenomenal. The Small House Policy was introduced in 1972. The Bill of Rights was enacted in 1991. The Basic Law has been in force for 23 years. And the lack of explanation for such a delay is simply glaring.

The prejudice caused by the delay to third parties and detriment to good administration is substantial and goes beyond the potential hardship to the NTIIs who currently own small houses.

It is well established that interest in good administration lies essentially in a regular flow of consistent decisions, made and published with reasonable dispatch; and in citizens knowing where they stand, and how they can order their affairs in the light of the relevant decision: AW v Director of Immigration [2016] 2 HKC 393 at [23]-[26]; R v Dairy Produce Quota Tribunal, ex p Caswell [1990] 2 AC 738 at 749H. Here, the Small House Policy has been consistently and regularly applied since 1972, which has affected many in the New Territories. In fact, between 1972 and 2018, a total of 111,995 applications were received, of which 8,657 applications were still being processed as at 30 June 2018. Many eligible NTIIs and their families have conducted affairs on the basis that the Small House Policy is a lawful policy, with an expectation of being able to build a small house in the future. The prejudice caused to them is on any view huge. Moreover, the Small House Policy involves important decisions of planning and scarce land allocation, which forms part of the land administration in the New Territories generally. The delay therefore also indirectly but adversely affects the interest in the land administration in the New Territories, which has been operating over the years on the basis that the Small House Policy is a valid and lawful policy.

On standing, the Court agreed with Mr Yu and Lord Pannick that the applicants did not have sufficient standing. They have not indicated any intention to build a small house. They do not suggest that they have any interest in any land, actual or potential, in the New Territories which is affected by the Small House Policy. They have not said that but for the Small House Policy, they would have gained some land or housing benefit. In short, they do not have any interest over and above that of an ordinary resident which give them standing to bring the judicial review.

In the circumstances, the Court would have refused relief on the ground of delay and lack of standing even if it were to hold that the Small House Policy was, wholly or partially, unconstitutional.

## 4. Striking, Unusual or Remarkable Aspects

Several aspects of this case are striking, unusual or remarkable:

**1. The Extensive Historical Survey:** The Court of Appeal conducted an extraordinarily detailed historical survey of the New Territories, going back to 1898 and beyond. The judgment spans over 130 paragraphs and includes twelve contextual matters, ranging from the status of NTIIs to the drafting materials of the Basic Law. This level of historical detail is unusual in constitutional litigation and reflects the complexity of the issues involved.

**2. The "Phenomenal" Delay:** The Court described the delay in bringing the challenge as "phenomenal." The Small House Policy was introduced in 1972, the Bill of Rights was enacted in 1991, and the Basic Law has been in force for 23 years. The applicants brought their challenge decades after the policy was implemented. The Court's characterization of the delay as "phenomenal" is striking and underscores the importance of legal certainty and the finality of administrative policies.

**3. The Number of Affected Applications:** The Court noted that between 1972 and 2018, a total of 111,995 applications were received, of which 8,657 applications were still being processed as of 30 June 2018. This staggering number illustrates the widespread reliance on the Small House Policy and the potential consequences of striking it down. The sheer scale of the policy's impact on land administration in the New Territories is remarkable.

**4. The CEDAW Reservations:** The case involved a nuanced analysis of the CEDAW reservations made by the UK and PRC Governments. The Court held that these reservations, which seek to ensure the continued application of laws enabling male indigenous villagers to exercise certain rights in respect of property, are a weighty aid to the interpretation of the Basic Law and the Bill of Rights. The interplay between international treaty reservations and constitutional interpretation is a sophisticated and unusual aspect of the case.

**5. The Coherence Principle:** The Court's application of the coherence principle to read Article 40 together with Articles 120 and 122, and with the CEDAW reservations, is a remarkable exercise in constitutional interpretation. The Court held that Article 40 must be construed to exclude a discrimination challenge based on other grounds, such as birth or social origin, to ensure a coherent constitutional scheme. This reasoning is novel and controversial, as it effectively shields the Small House Policy from constitutional challenge despite its inherently discriminatory nature.

**6. The Rejection of the Tracing Exercise:** The Court of Appeal rejected the tracing exercise adopted by the Judge, holding that Article 40 does not entail a historical tracing exercise to determine whether an asserted right or interest is a traditional right or interest of the NTIIs. Instead, the Court held that whether a right or interest is traditional for the purpose of Article 40 is to be determined by reference to the state of affairs in April 1990. This departure from the Judge's approach is significant and controversial, as it simplifies the analysis but potentially broadens the scope of protection under Article 40.

**7. The Recognition of the Ding Rights as "Rights and Interests":** The Court's holding that the Ding Rights, even if only a right to apply for a small house grant under a government policy, constitute "rights and interests" for the purpose of Article 40 is remarkable. The Court relied on the doctrine of legitimate expectation and the principle of good administration to hold that the Small House Policy creates legal obligations on the part of the Government. This reasoning elevates an administrative policy to the level of constitutional protection, which is unusual and potentially far-reaching.

**8. The Dissolution of the Land Commission:** The Joint Declaration provided for the setting up of the Land Commission, which was to be dissolved on 30 June 1997. This detail, while minor, adds to the historical richness of the case and underscores the transitional arrangements surrounding the resumption of sovereignty.

**9. The Obiter Remark in Chan Wah:** The Court noted that Li CJ's remark in Secretary for Justice v Chan Wah (2000) 3 HKCFAR 459 at p.477D-F that Article 40 covered the benefits relating to land granted to male NTIIs under the Small House Policy is obiter. This acknowledgment is significant because it clarifies that the Court of Appeal's decision is not bound by the Chief Justice's remark, and it highlights the ongoing development of the law in this area.

**10. The Lack of Legal Challenge Until 2019:** The fact that the Small House Policy had never been subject to any legal challenge since 1972 until the proceedings below is striking. It underscores the long-standing acceptance of the policy and the potential disruption that a successful challenge would cause. The Court's emphasis on this point reinforces the importance of legal certainty and the consequences of delayed challenges.

**11. The Use of the Term "Ding Rights":** The Court adopted the term "Ding Rights" for easy reference, noting that in Chinese, "Ding Rights" is "丁權" and "Ding" means "丁", a male person. This linguistic detail is a small but interesting aspect of the case, reflecting the cultural and historical context of the policy.

**12. The Scale of the Judgment:** The judgment is exceptionally long and detailed, spanning over 130 paragraphs and covering a wide range of historical, legal and constitutional issues. The Court's thoroughness is remarkable and reflects the complexity and importance of the case. The judgment is likely to be a landmark decision in Hong Kong constitutional law.

**13. The Controversial Outcome:** The outcome of the case—upholding the Small House Policy in its entirety despite its inherently discriminatory nature—is controversial. The Court's reasoning that Article 40 shields the policy from constitutional challenge based on discrimination is likely to be debated and critiqued. The case raises important questions about the balance between constitutional protection of traditional rights and the principle of equality.

**14. The Role of the Joint Declaration:** The Court's reliance on the Joint Declaration and Annex III to interpret Article 40 is significant. The Joint Declaration is a bilateral international agreement, and its use as an interpretive aid to the Basic Law is a sophisticated and unusual aspect of the case. The Court's analysis of the agreement reached by the UK and PRC Governments on small house grants is a key part of its reasoning.

**15. The Prejudice to Third Parties:** The Court's emphasis on the prejudice caused by the delay to third parties and detriment to good administration is striking. The Court noted that many eligible NTIIs and their families have conducted affairs on the basis that the Small House Policy is a lawful policy, with an expectation of being able to build a small house in the future. The prejudice caused to them is on any view huge. This consideration is a powerful reminder of the real-world consequences of constitutional litigation.

In conclusion, Kwok Cheuk Kin and Another v Director of Lands and Others [2021] HKCA 54 is a remarkable case that raises fundamental questions about constitutional interpretation, the protection of traditional rights, and the balance between equality and cultural heritage. The Court of Appeal's judgment is thorough, sophisticated and controversial, and it is likely to have a lasting impact on Hong Kong constitutional law. [end of text]


